  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "protein-topic-60fa",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and publications report single learned models handling multiple targets, states, or property sets for drug and protein design.",
  "synthesis": "Across these items, the dominant thread is learned generative or amortized modeling applied to molecules and proteins, in which one model predicts responses or proposes designs under several conditions. PerturbPFN, a PFN-style model trained on synthetic episodes, predicts responses to unseen chemical perturbations and infers targets and strengths. Chamaileon unifies multi-target and multi-state binder design through cross-context modeling, co-design training, and mixed-path sampling. DeepBioisostere autonomously selects and substitutes molecular fragments for multi-property optimization, while Mol-CADiff generates molecules from text prompts using causal attention within a diffusion framework. Raygun and ProteinGuide extend this pattern to protein sequence design, with ProteinGuide conditioning pretrained sequence generators on auxiliary properties. What is new is movement from single-objective, single-state prediction toward unified models that handle heterogeneous contexts, conditions, or property sets. The items differ in modality and task, so this describes a dominant pattern rather than a method shared by all items.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-31"
}
